---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHeader from "../../components/PageHeader.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Why we built Reading the Law: an independent, no-nonsense guide to the apprenticeship path to becoming an attorney, and the matchmaking platform we're building next."
>
  <PageHeader eyebrow="About" title="Why this site exists" />

  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 py-16">
    <article class="prose-legal">
      <p class="text-lg">
        Law school now routinely costs six figures, and for a lot of capable people, that's simply not a
        reasonable trade for a career. Most of them never learn that four U.S. states still let you
        become a fully licensed attorney by apprenticing under a practicing lawyer or judge instead,
        because the information is scattered across dense rule text, decades-old forum threads, and the
        occasional viral news story about a celebrity attempting it.
      </p>
      <p>
        We built Reading the Law to put the real rules, requirements, and trade-offs for every active
        apprenticeship state in one honest, current place, including the parts that don't make for a good
        headline, like lower bar passage rates and limited license portability.
      </p>

      <h2>What we're building next</h2>
      <p>
        The information on this site solves the "I didn't know this was possible" problem. It doesn't solve
        the next one: every program requires you to find your own supervising attorney or judge, and there's
        currently no structured way to do that beyond your personal network. We're building a
        <a href="/matchmaking/">matchmaking platform</a> to connect aspiring apprentices directly with
        attorneys and judges open to sponsoring one, because we think that's the real barrier keeping this
        path from being viable for more people, not the studying itself.
      </p>

      <h2>What this site is (and isn't)</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>We are not a law firm, a state bar, a board of bar examiners, or any government body.</li>
        <li>We don't provide legal advice, and nothing on this site should be treated as a substitute for confirming current rules directly with the relevant state authority.</li>
        <li>We aren't affiliated with, endorsed by, or acting on behalf of California, Vermont, Virginia, Washington, Maine, or New York's bar admissions authorities.</li>
        <li>We're an independent project built for people seriously evaluating this path, not marketing copy for any bar-prep company, law school, or admissions consultant.</li>
      </ul>

      <Callout tone="info" title="Found something out of date?">
        Bar admission rules change. If you notice something on this site that no longer matches your
        state's official rule, we want to fix it. Every page links to the official governing body so you
        can verify independently in the meantime.
      </Callout>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
